  "slug": "mitochondrial-acid-2d6e",
  "summary": "Two preprints identify SLC25A35 as the mitochondrial branched-chain keto acid carrier, with import shaping hepatic BCKA handling and adipocyte lipogenesis.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that mitochondrial substrate entry and organelle remodeling set tissue metabolic output. Two preprints identify SLC25A35 as the mitochondrial carrier for branched-chain keto acids (BCKAs). One also names MCT1 and MCT2 as cellular entry transporters and reports that import limits hepatic BCKA handling; the other links BCKA uptake to adipocyte lipogenesis and fatty acid metabolism. MTCH2 supports mitochondrial remodeling, respiration and fatty acid oxidation in muscle satellite cells. Myeloid mDIC deletion shifts adipose macrophages to an efferocytosis-associated state with preserved respiration. In KPC cachexia mice, muscle OPA1 overexpression or BNIP3 knockdown partly preserved muscle mass and function, sex-dependently. Dual Hadha and Hadhb mRNA delivery restores fatty acid oxidation in a trifunctional protein deficiency model. The new element is the transporter-level account of BCKA import, from cell entry to mitochondrial uptake.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
